    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The survey recorded who held each piece of land after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>, what it was worth and what dues the king was owed.</li><li>Its date is traditionally 1086, but some historians argue that the book itself was compiled later, under William II or as late as 1098 to 1114.</li><li>Kept first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> and then at Westminster, it is now at the National Archives and was printed in full in 1783.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The English called it Domesday because, as Richard FitzNeal explained around 1179, there was no appeal from its record, just as there is none from the Last Judgment. The word doom then meant law or judgment, not disaster.</p></aside>
    </section>

